    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "A key issue in selection theory is the expected time required for a given amount of allele-frequency change. Assuming that s and sh are small (i.e., there is weak selection, so that $ \\overline{W} \\simeq 1 $), we can ignore $ \\overline{W} $ in Equation 5.1.c as a first-order approximation (see Example 5.1). Equation 5.1c then shows that the change in allele frequency p under weak selection is approximated by the differential equation $$ \\frac{dp}{dt}=sp(1-p)[1+h(1-2p)] $$",
      "source_page": 113,
      "source_pages": [
        113,
        137
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_chapter5:p004:b11",
        "Evolution_chapter5:p028:b8",
        "Evolution_chapter5:p004:b12",
        "Evolution_chapter5:p004:b13"
      ],
